The night darkens, and the outlines of centuries-old trees nearby become clearer. You hear the first birdsong, accompanied by the chirring of insects from the grass. You need to keep moving; today, it's crucial to exit this forest and return home at all costs. The overnight stay clearly demonstrated that it's better not to linger here.

You hastily pack your belongings and begin to march, attempting to follow some semblance of a path trodden by wild animals. At least there's hope that it will lead you to a water source, and from there, you can find your way out.

You've been walking for quite some time, but the forest seems unending. Finally, the gaps between the trees widen, and you come to an edge. You let out an involuntary scream of joy; ahead, in the lowland, you can discern a vague silhouette of a body of water. Firstly, you need to reach it. The animal trail leads to another wood, smaller than the one you've just passed, consisting only of short shrubs. This underbrush is surrounded by fields where tall grass grows. Gusts of wind sway the plants on the fields, making it seem like you're close to the vast lake. It's time to choose the best route to the water.